    <node id="UNITED STATES">
      <data key="d0">UNITED STATES</data>
      <data key="d1">UNITED STATES</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The United States is a country with significant relevance in cancer epidemiology, prevention, treatment, and research. In the U.S., prostate cancer is the second most common cancer among American males, with notable racial and ethnic disparities: Black males are more likely than white males to develop prostate cancer and are often diagnosed at an earlier age with more aggressive or advanced disease. These disparities in prostate cancer incidence, aggressiveness, and death rates are documented in the United States. The country has also achieved much lower rates of cervical cancer due to the widespread use of Pap smears, and an HPV vaccine protecting against nine different strains of the virus, including the highest-risk strains, is available in the U.S. Approximately 3 million cases of basal cell skin cancer are diagnosed in the United States every year.

The United States has faced a shortage of BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) for several years, which has affected the availability of intravesical BCG therapy for bladder cancer. In terms of cancer care infrastructure, the United States is home to the NCCN Cancer Centers—the leading cancer centers that form the National Comprehensive Cancer Network—as well as the National Cancer Institute (NCI), the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM), and the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). These organizations are located or operate within the United States and serve as key resources for cancer care and clinical trials. Many clinical trial resources and agencies are based in the U.S., and the NCCN Foundation, operating in the United States, works to fund promising doctors. Thus, the United States plays a central role in cancer surveillance, prevention, treatment innovation, and clinical research, while also confronting challenges such as drug shortages and persistent racial disparities in cancer outcomes. 
**Relationships:**
  - Source: NCCN CANCER CENTERS
  - Target: UNITED STATES
  - Description: NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment and research institutions located throughout the United States. These centers are recognized as a key resource for patients and providers seeking information about clinical trials. Specifically, the NCCN Cancer Centers are listed under the category "In the United States" as a resource for finding clinical trials. This listing indicates that the centers serve as a designated point of reference for individuals looking to identify and access clinical trial opportunities within the country. The descriptions consistently emphasize that NCCN Cancer Centers are based in the United States and function as a practical tool for clinical trial discovery, making the information accessible and relevant to a U.S. audience. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers comprise a U.S.-based network, and they are formally indexed as a clinical trial finding resource within the United States, ensuring that patients and clinicians can readily locate trials through these centers. 
  - Source: UNITED STATES
  - Target: NCCN CANCER CENTERS
  - Description: NCCN Cancer Centers are located in the United States. 

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: UNITED STATES
Description: The United States is a country with significant relevance in cancer epidemiology, prevention, treatment, and research. In the U.S., prostate cancer is the second most common cancer among American males, with notable racial and ethnic disparities: Black males are more likely than white males to develop prostate cancer and are often diagnosed at an earlier age with more aggressive or advanced disease. These disparities in prostate cancer incidence, aggressiveness, and death rates are documented in the United States. The country has also achieved much lower rates of cervical cancer due to the widespread use of Pap smears, and an HPV vaccine protecting against nine different strains of the virus, including the highest-risk strains, is available in the U.S. Approximately 3 million cases of basal cell skin cancer are diagnosed in the United States every year.

The United States has faced a shortage of BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) for several years, which has affected the availability of intravesical BCG therapy for bladder cancer. In terms of cancer care infrastructure, the United States is home to the NCCN Cancer Centers—the leading cancer centers that form the National Comprehensive Cancer Network—as well as the National Cancer Institute (NCI), the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM), and the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). These organizations are located or operate within the United States and serve as key resources for cancer care and clinical trials. Many clinical trial resources and agencies are based in the U.S., and the NCCN Foundation, operating in the United States, works to fund promising doctors. Thus, the United States plays a central role in cancer surveillance, prevention, treatment innovation, and clinical research, while also confronting challenges such as drug shortages and persistent racial disparities in cancer outcomes. 
