      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for protecting public health by regulating and approving new drugs, medical devices, and treatment approaches. The FDA may grant approval for these products after they are found to be safe and effective in clinical trials, specifically following successful Phase 3 clinical trial results. In addition to its role in initial approvals, the FDA also monitors FDA-approved treatments during Phase 4 trials to ensure their ongoing safety and effectiveness in real-world use. This comprehensive oversight makes the FDA a central authority in the U.S. healthcare system. 
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data.

