      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: HPV INFECTION
Description: HPV infection, also referred to as infection with human papillomavirus, is an extremely common sexually transmitted infection. In most cases, the infection clears on its own without requiring any treatment. However, HPV infection can sometimes cause abnormal tissue growth, and in certain situations, it may lead to cancer. Specifically, long-term infection with high-risk HPV types is a known cause of cervical cancer and is also associated with other HPV-related cancers. The HPV vaccine is a key preventive measure, as it can prevent new infections, but it does not treat infections that are already present. Overall, HPV infection is a widespread and typically self-resolving condition; nevertheless, persistent infection with high-risk strains carries a significant risk of serious complications, making prevention through vaccination particularly important.
Relationships:
  - Source: HPV INFECTION
  - Target: HPV-RELATED CANCER
  - Description: HPV infection can cause abnormal tissue growth and sometimes HPV-related cancer.
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: HPV INFECTION
  - Description: Most cervical cancers are caused by long-term infection with human papillomavirus.
  - Source: HPV INFECTION
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: HPV infection is associated with anal cancer; the text links HPV-related cancers and prevention of new HPV infections.
  - Source: HPV INFECTION
  - Target: THROAT CANCER
  - Description: Throat cancer is most often related to HPV infection.
  - Source: HPV INFECTION
  - Target: CERVICAL DYSPLASIA
  - Description: HPV can cause long-term cell changes; cervical dysplasia is an abnormal cell change of the cervix.

