  - Target: ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Ultrasound is often the first imaging test used to look for ovarian cancer.

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEEDLE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">NEEDLE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">NEEDLE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Needle biopsy is a medical procedure in which a needle is used to extract a sample of tissue or fluid from the body for diagnostic testing. It is often performed on a lymph node, allowing pathologists to examine the sample for abnormalities such as infection or cancer. The procedure is also known by another name: fine-needle aspiration (FNA), reflecting its minimally invasive nature and use of a thin needle to collect cellular material. When required, a needle biopsy may be carried out by a breast diagnostic radiologist, particularly in cases involving suspicious breast findings. In this context, the radiologist uses imaging guidance to precisely place the needle and obtain an adequate sample for evaluation. Thus, needle biopsy is a versatile and commonly used diagnostic tool that can be performed on various body sites, with a lymph node being one example, and is closely associated with fine-needle aspiration and radiological expertise.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: NEEDLE BIOPSY
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Needle biopsy is a form of biopsy
  - Source: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Target: NEEDLE BIOPSY
  - Description: A lymph node biopsy is performed using a needle biopsy to take a sample from the lymph node</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPINAL TAP">
      <data key="d0">SPINAL TAP</data>
      <data key="d1">SPINAL TAP</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">SPINAL TAP is another name for a lumbar puncture, a medical procedure used to remove cerebrospinal fluid from around the spine or brain. Specifically, the procedure is performed in the lower back, where a needle is inserted to access the spinal canal and collect fluid for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes.
Relationships:
  - Source: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Target: SPINAL TAP
  - Description: A lumbar puncture, also known as a spinal tap, is a medical procedure. Both names—lumbar puncture and spinal tap—refer to the same diagnostic and therapeutic technique. The procedure involves inserting a needle into the lower back to access the subarachnoid space in order to collect cerebrospinal fluid for analysis, measure pressure, or administer medication. It is performed by a trained healthcare professional and is commonly used to help diagnose conditions affecting the central nervous system, such as infections, inflammation, or bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
