    <node id="LEUKEMIA">
      <data key="d0">LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d1">LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">LEUKEMIA is a cancer of blood-forming tissues, characterized by the abnormal proliferation of blood cells. This disease can spread beyond the bloodstream to other parts of the body, including the lymph nodes, liver, spleen, and skin, and, in rare cases, it may affect the lining of the brain and spinal cord. Additionally, leukemia can travel to the cerebrospinal fluid surrounding the spine or brain, where it may cause symptoms such as headaches, neck pain, and sensitivity to light. The development of leukemia can be influenced by inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes, which contribute to an individual's genetic risk for the disease.
Relationships:
  - Source: LEUKEMIA
  - Target: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Description: Lumbar puncture removes spinal fluid to test for leukemia cells in the spinal fluid
  - Source: LEUKEMIA
  - Target: LEUKEMIC MENINGITIS
  - Description: Leukemia can cause leukemic meningitis, swelling of the outer layer of the brain
  - Source: LEUKEMIA
  - Target: HEADACHE
  - Description: Leukemia can cause headaches when it travels to the fluid around the spine or brain</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
