      <data key="d3">Endorectal ultrasound, also known as EUS, is a diagnostic imaging test that uses a small probe inserted into the rectum. The probe emits sound waves to create detailed images of the pelvis, allowing physicians to determine the extent of cancer in the area. It can also be used to guide biopsy procedures. In addition to its role in initial staging and diagnosis, endorectal ultrasound serves as an important surveillance tool after treatments such as endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) and transanal local excision. For patients undergoing surveillance, the test is recommended every 3 to 6 months for the first two years, and then every 6 months through the fifth year following treatment.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
Description: Endorectal ultrasound, also known as EUS, is a diagnostic imaging test that uses a small probe inserted into the rectum. The probe emits sound waves to create detailed images of the pelvis, allowing physicians to determine the extent of cancer in the area. It can also be used to guide biopsy procedures. In addition to its role in initial staging and diagnosis, endorectal ultrasound serves as an important surveillance tool after treatments such as endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) and transanal local excision. For patients undergoing surveillance, the test is recommended every 3 to 6 months for the first two years, and then every 6 months through the fifth year following treatment.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data</data>
    </node>
