    <node id="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM">
      <data key="d0">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d1">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A digital rectal exam (DRE) is a physical examination procedure in which a doctor inserts a gloved, lubricated finger into the anus and rectum to feel the prostate gland. The exam is used to check the size and texture of the prostate, as well as to detect any irregular or hardened areas that could be signs of cancer. It is a simple test that can suggest the presence of prostate cancer and may lead to further diagnostic testing. The procedure is also used for diagnosis, staging, and follow-up of prostate conditions, including after treatment. For individuals who have received treatment for cancer that involves lymph nodes only, digital rectal exams are recommended every 3 to 6 months for 5 years following treatment. An abnormal result from a digital rectal exam can be a sign of prostate cancer, prompting additional evaluation. Overall, the DRE is a straightforward, valuable tool for assessing prostate health and detecting potential abnormalities.
Relationships:
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION
  - Description: The physical exam is performed together with digital rectal examination.</data>
    </node>
