    <node id="LYMPH NODE EXAM">
      <data key="d0">LYMPH NODE EXAM</data>
      <data key="d1">LYMPH NODE EXAM</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Lymph node exam refers to a clinical evaluation of the inguinal (groin) lymph nodes, performed by palpation to detect any enlargement. This examination is used in the staging of anal cancer and is an important component of patient follow-up. Inguinal lymph node exams are recommended every 3 to 6 months for 5 years after initial treatment, and the same schedule applies after treatment for recurrence. Thus, the exam serves both diagnostic and surveillance purposes, helping to monitor for potential spread or recurrence of disease. The exam is a routine, non-invasive procedure that involves manual palpation of the lymph nodes in the groin area.
Relationships:
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: LYMPH NODE EXAM
  - Description: Surveillance includes regular inguinal lymph node examinations as a recommended component. Specifically, these exams are advised every 3 to 6 months for a period of 5 years.
  - Source: LYMPH NODE EXAM
  - Target: DOCTOR
  - Description: Your doctor examines lymph nodes in the groin by palpation
  - Source: LYMPH NODE EXAM
  - Target: GROIN
  - Description: The doctor examines lymph nodes in the groin by pressing on the surface of the skin
  - Source: ANAL SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
  - Target: LYMPH NODE EXAM
  - Description: Lymph node exam is recommended after diagnosis of anal squamous cell carcinoma
  - Source: LYMPH NODE EXAM
  - Target: INGUINAL LYMPH NODES
  - Description: Lymph node exam checks the inguinal lymph nodes in the groin</data>
    </node>
