    <node id="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER">
      <data key="d0">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
      <data key="d1">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER is a medical professional who delivers comprehensive patient care. This clinician, which includes doctors and other medical professionals, performs thorough physical examinations and checks vital signs, body systems, and lymph nodes. The health care provider also assesses height, weight, lungs, heart, eyes, ears, nose, throat, organs, and skin. In addition to conducting exams, the provider gives patients important information about next steps and treatment options. The health care provider (HCP) receives imaging reports from the radiologist and discusses the results with the patient, ensuring clear communication and follow-up care.
Relationships: (None listed)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLINICAL STAGING">
      <data key="d0">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Clinical staging is the rating of cancer assigned before any treatment is initiated. It is determined based on a physical exam, biopsy, and imaging tests, as well as other diagnostic evaluations. This staging system provides a baseline assessment of the extent of the disease prior to therapy. Examples of clinical staging designations include cN1, cN2, and cM0, where the prefix "c" indicates that the staging is clinical, based on initial examination and tests rather than surgical findings. The clinical stage helps guide treatment decisions and serves as a reference point for evaluating the effectiveness of subsequent interventions.

**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data.

---</data>
    </node>
