    <node id="SOURCE: CLINICAL TRIALS → TARGET: RESEARCH TEAM — CLINICAL TRIALS ARE RESEARCH STUDIES CONDUCTED TO EVALUATE MEDICAL, SURGICAL, OR BEHAVIORAL INTERVENTIONS. THESE TRIALS ARE MANAGED BY A DEDICATED RESEARCH TEAM, WHICH PLAYS A CENTRAL ROLE IN THE STUDY PROCESS. THE RESEARCH TEAM IS RESPONSIBLE FOR REVIEWING THE STUDY DETAILS WITH PARTICIPANTS, ENSURING THEY UNDERSTAND THE PURPOSE, PROCEDURES, RISKS, AND POTENTIAL BENEFITS BEFORE ENROLLMENT. IN ADDITION TO GUIDING PARTICIPANTS THROUGH THE INFORMED CONSENT PROCESS, THE RESEARCH TEAM ACTIVELY HELPS COLLECT RESEARCH DATA THROUGHOUT THE TRIAL. THEY ALSO COORDINATE PATIENT CARE, WORKING ALONGSIDE HEALTHCARE PROVIDERS TO ENSURE THAT PARTICIPANTS RECEIVE APPROPRIATE MEDICAL ATTENTION AND THAT THE TRIAL PROTOCOL IS FOLLOWED. THUS, THE RESEARCH TEAM BRIDGES THE GAP BETWEEN CLINICAL CARE AND RESEARCH, SUPPORTING BOTH DATA INTEGRITY AND PARTICIPANT WELL-BEING. TOGETHER, CLINICAL TRIALS AND THE RESEARCH TEAM FORM AN INTEGRATED FRAMEWORK FOR ADVANCING MEDICAL KNOWLEDGE WHILE MAINTAINING PATIENT SAFETY AND CARE COORDINATION.">
