    <node id="U.S. FDA">
      <data key="d0">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The U.S. FDA, also known as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration, is a federal agency of the United States government. As a government organization, it is responsible for regulating and approving medical treatments, including investigational treatments that arise from clinical trials. The agency evaluates drugs, devices, and treatment approaches and grants approval once they are found to be both safe and effective in clinical trials. In addition to standard approvals, the U.S. FDA may also approve investigational treatments derived from clinical trials, ensuring that new medical interventions meet rigorous standards before reaching patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: U.S. FDA
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: A drug, device, or treatment approach found safe and effective in a clinical trial may be approved by the U.S. FDA

---</data>
