    <node id="NAUSEA">
      <data key="d0">NAUSEA</data>
      <data key="d1">NAUSEA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Nausea is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatments. It frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, a standard form of radiotherapy. Beyond this specific technique, nausea is also a common side effect of radiation therapy in general, as well as targeted therapy. Additionally, nausea is a side effect that the chemotherapy drug irinotecan tends to cause. Thus, patients undergoing external beam radiation, broader radiation therapy, targeted therapy, or treatment with irinotecan may experience nausea as an adverse effect.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: NAUSEA
Description: Nausea is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatments. It frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, a standard form of radiotherapy. Beyond this specific technique, nausea is also a common side effect of radiation therapy in general, as well as targeted therapy. Additionally, nausea is a side effect that the chemotherapy drug irinotecan tends to cause. Thus, patients undergoing external beam radiation, broader radiation therapy, targeted therapy, or treatment with irinotecan may experience nausea as an adverse effect.
Relationships: None listed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATIGUE">
      <data key="d0">FATIGUE</data>
      <data key="d1">FATIGUE</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">FATIGUE is a recognized side effect of cancer treatments. It commonly occurs during the course of external beam radiation therapy, typically appearing around the fifth to sixth week of treatment. Fatigue may also begin after radiation treatment has ended and can persist for several weeks. In addition to radiation therapy, fatigue is associated with other treatment modalities, including targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and high-dose chemotherapy. This side effect is shared across multiple cancer treatment approaches and can affect patients at various stages of their treatment journey.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: FATIGUE
Description: FATIGUE is a recognized side effect of cancer treatments. It commonly occurs during the course of external beam radiation therapy, typically appearing around the fifth to sixth week of treatment. Fatigue may also begin after radiation treatment has ended and can persist for several weeks. In addition to radiation therapy, fatigue is associated with other treatment modalities, including targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and high-dose chemotherapy. This side effect is shared across multiple cancer treatment approaches and can affect patients at various stages of their treatment journey.
Relationships: None listed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIDE EFFECTS">
      <data key="d0">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d1">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">SIDE EFFECTS are unintended effects that can arise from medical treatment, most notably external beam radiation therapy (EBRT). Common adverse effects of EBRT include fatigue, skin redness and irritation, diarrhea, nausea, and frequent or painful urination. These effects may be monitored during treatment—for example, heart tests can be used to track treatment-related side effects—and supportive care is often provided to address and manage them. Together, these descriptions highlight that side effects are both a known set of physical reactions to radiation therapy and a broader concept encompassing any unintended consequences of treatment that require monitoring and supportive intervention.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: SIDE EFFECTS
Description: SIDE EFFECTS are unintended effects that can arise from medical treatment, most notably external beam radiation therapy (EBRT). Common adverse effects of EBRT include fatigue, skin redness and irritation, diarrhea, nausea, and frequent or painful urination. These effects may be monitored during treatment—for example, heart tests can be used to track treatment-related side effects—and supportive care is often provided to address and manage them. Together, these descriptions highlight that side effects are both a known set of physical reactions to radiation therapy and a broader concept encompassing any unintended consequences of treatment that require monitoring and supportive intervention.
Relationships: None listed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIARRHEA">
      <data key="d0">DIARRHEA</data>
      <data key="d1">DIARRHEA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">DIARRHEA is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatment modalities. Specifically, it frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, particularly when radiation is directed at the abdomen or pelvis. Additionally, diarrhea is a well-documented adverse effect of targeted therapy, a class of drugs designed to interfere with specific molecules involved in tumor growth. Furthermore, the chemotherapy agent irinotecan is known to commonly cause diarrhea as a side effect. In summary, diarrhea is a shared and clinically significant side effect across external beam radiation therapy, targeted therapy, and treatment with irinotecan.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
