    <node id="SURGERY → CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">SURGERY → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">SURGERY → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are two primary treatment modalities used for ovarian cancer, and a gynecologic oncologist is an expert in both. These treatments are often combined in a coordinated sequence, and the order in which they are administered depends on the extent of the cancer at diagnosis.

CHEMOTHERAPY may be given before SURGERY, in which case it is referred to as neoadjuvant therapy. This approach is typically used when the cancer has spread too extensively to be removed safely or completely by initial SURGERY. By shrinking the tumors first, neoadjuvant chemotherapy can make subsequent surgical removal more feasible and effective. Alternatively, CHEMOTHERAPY may be given after SURGERY, known as primary or adjuvant chemotherapy. This is used to eliminate any remaining cancer cells that may not have been visible or removable during the operation. Adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended after SURGERY for most stage 1 ovarian cancers, as well as for stages 2, 3, and 4.

In summary, SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are both essential and complementary tools in treating ovarian cancer. Their optimal sequence is determined by disease stage and resectability: if SURGERY cannot be performed first due to extensive spread, CHEMOTHERAPY is administered before SURGERY; otherwise, SURGERY is performed first and followed by CHEMOTHERAPY. The combined expertise of a gynecologic oncologist covers both modalities, ensuring that patients receive the most appropriate and individualized treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: SURGERY → CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are two primary treatment modalities used for ovarian cancer, and a gynecologic oncologist is an expert in both. These treatments are often combined in a coordinated sequence, and the order in which they are administered depends on the extent of the cancer at diagnosis.

CHEMOTHERAPY may be given before SURGERY, in which case it is referred to as neoadjuvant therapy. This approach is typically used when the cancer has spread too extensively to be removed safely or completely by initial SURGERY. By shrinking the tumors first, neoadjuvant chemotherapy can make subsequent surgical removal more feasible and effective. Alternatively, CHEMOTHERAPY may be given after SURGERY, known as primary or adjuvant chemotherapy. This is used to eliminate any remaining cancer cells that may not have been visible or removable during the operation. Adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended after SURGERY for most stage 1 ovarian cancers, as well as for stages 2, 3, and 4.

In summary, SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are both essential and complementary tools in treating ovarian cancer. Their optimal sequence is determined by disease stage and resectability: if SURGERY cannot be performed first due to extensive spread, CHEMOTHERAPY is administered before SURGERY; otherwise, SURGERY is performed first and followed by CHEMOTHERAPY. The combined expertise of a gynecologic oncologist covers both modalities, ensuring that patients receive the most appropriate and individualized treatment plan.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CERVICAL CANCER → CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">CERVICAL CANCER → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">CERVICAL CANCER → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CERVICAL CANCER → CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.</data>
    </node>
