      <data key="d1">TREATMENT → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiation—a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: TREATMENT → CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiation—a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTHERAPY → RADIATION THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMOTHERAPY → RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">CHEMOTHERAPY → RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy—termed chemoradiation—represents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CHEMOTHERAPY → RADIATION THERAPY
Description: Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy—termed chemoradiation—represents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RADIATION THERAPY → CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">RADIATION THERAPY → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">RADIATION THERAPY → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Radiation therapy and chemotherapy are two cancer treatment modalities that are often used together or compared. Chemotherapy is sometimes added to radiation therapy to enhance the overall treatment effect. In particular, chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use in combination with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer. When the two are compared as standalone treatment options, radiation therapy may be more likely than chemotherapy to damage the nervous system.</data>
