    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTHERAPY → OVARIAN CANCER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMOTHERAPY → OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">CHEMOTHERAPY → OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CHEMOTHERAPY → OVARIAN CANCER
Description: Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMORADIATION → CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMORADIATION → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">CHEMORADIATION → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CHEMORADIATION → CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTHERAPY → CANCER TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMOTHERAPY → CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">CHEMOTHERAPY → CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CHEMOTHERAPY → CANCER TREATMENT
Description: Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IN SUMMARY, SURGERY AND CHEMOTHERAPY ARE BOTH ESSENTIAL AND COMPLEMENTARY TOOLS IN TREATING OVARIAN CANCER. THEIR OPTIMAL SEQUENCE IS DETERMINED BY DISEASE STAGE AND RESECTABILITY: IF SURGERY CANNOT BE PERFORMED FIRST DUE TO EXTENSIVE SPREAD, CHEMOTHERAPY IS ADMINISTERED BEFORE SURGERY; OTHERWISE, SURGERY IS PERFORMED FIRST AND FOLLOWED BY CHEMOTHERAPY. THE COMBINED EXPERTISE OF A GYNECOLOGIC ONCOLOGIST COVERS BOTH MODALITIES, ENSURING THAT PATIENTS RECEIVE THE MOST APPROPRIATE AND INDIVIDUALIZED TREATMENT PLAN.">
      <data key="d0">IN SUMMARY, SURGERY AND CHEMOTHERAPY ARE BOTH ESSENTIAL AND COMPLEMENTARY TOOLS IN TREATING OVARIAN CANCER. THEIR OPTIMAL SEQUENCE IS DETERMINED BY DISEASE STAGE AND RESECTABILITY: IF SURGERY CANNOT BE PERFORMED FIRST DUE TO EXTENSIVE SPREAD, CHEMOTHERAPY IS ADMINISTERED BEFORE SURGERY; OTHERWISE, SURGERY IS PERFORMED FIRST AND FOLLOWED BY CHEMOTHERAPY. THE COMBINED EXPERTISE OF A GYNECOLOGIC ONCOLOGIST COVERS BOTH MODALITIES, ENSURING THAT PATIENTS RECEIVE THE MOST APPROPRIATE AND INDIVIDUALIZED TREATMENT PLAN.</data>
