      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team talks with the patient about the types of chemotherapy, doses, timing, and side effects
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: The care team helps manage side effects such as nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiation—a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy—termed chemoradiation—represents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Radiation therapy and chemotherapy are two cancer treatment modalities that are often used together or compared. Chemotherapy is sometimes added to radiation therapy to enhance the overall treatment effect. In particular, chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use in combination with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer. When the two are compared as standalone treatment options, radiation therapy may be more likely than chemotherapy to damage the nervous system.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy uses anti-cancer medicines that travel in the bloodstream to reach cells throughout the body, including cancer cells
