ENTITY: SURVIVORSHIP CARE FOR CANCER-RELATED LATE AND LONG-TERM EFFECTS
Description: This is an NCCN patient guideline/resource for cancer survivors that addresses late and long-term effects of cancer treatment
Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: SURVIVORSHIP CARE FOR CANCER-RELATED LATE AND LONG-TERM EFFECTS
  - Description: NCCN publishes and provides this resource at NCCN.org/patientguidelines

ENTITY: NAUSEA
Description: Nausea is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatments. It frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, a standard form of radiotherapy. Beyond this specific technique, nausea is also a common side effect of radiation therapy in general, as well as targeted therapy. Additionally, nausea is a side effect that the chemotherapy drug irinotecan tends to cause. Thus, patients undergoing external beam radiation, broader radiation therapy, targeted therapy, or treatment with irinotecan may experience nausea as an adverse effect.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: FATIGUE
Description: FATIGUE is a recognized side effect of cancer treatments. It commonly occurs during the course of external beam radiation therapy, typically appearing around the fifth to sixth week of treatment. Fatigue may also begin after radiation treatment has ended and can persist for several weeks. In addition to radiation therapy, fatigue is associated with other treatment modalities, including targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and high-dose chemotherapy. This side effect is shared across multiple cancer treatment approaches and can affect patients at various stages of their treatment journey.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: SIDE EFFECTS
Description: SIDE EFFECTS are unintended effects that can arise from medical treatment, most notably external beam radiation therapy (EBRT). Common adverse effects of EBRT include fatigue, skin redness and irritation, diarrhea, nausea, and frequent or painful urination. These effects may be monitored during treatment—for example, heart tests can be used to track treatment-related side effects—and supportive care is often provided to address and manage them. Together, these descriptions highlight that side effects are both a known set of physical reactions to radiation therapy and a broader concept encompassing any unintended consequences of treatment that require monitoring and supportive intervention.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: DIARRHEA
Description: DIARRHEA is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatment modalities. Specifically, it frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, particularly when radiation is directed at the abdomen or pelvis. Additionally, diarrhea is a well-documented adverse effect of targeted therapy, a class of drugs designed to interfere with specific molecules involved in tumor growth. Furthermore, the chemotherapy agent irinotecan is known to commonly cause diarrhea as a side effect. In summary, diarrhea is a shared and clinically significant side effect across external beam radiation therapy, targeted therapy, and treatment with irinotecan.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: CHILLS
Description: Chills are a common side effect of targeted therapy.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: RADIATION
Description: RADIATION is a cancer treatment that is often combined with chemotherapy, a combination known as chemoradiation. As a type of cancer treatment, radiation is frequently mentioned alongside chemotherapy; the specific type of treatment administered influences the side effects a patient may experience.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: ALLERGIC REACTIONS
Description: Allergic reactions are a common side effect of targeted therapy.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: FOLFCIS
Description: A chemotherapy regimen containing 5-FU, leucovorin, and cisplatin
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: DCF
Description: A chemotherapy regimen containing modified docetaxel, cisplatin, and fluorouracil
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: HAIR LOSS
Description: Hair loss is a side effect that irinotecan tends to cause.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: CONSTIPATION
Description: Constipation is a possible side effect of immunotherapy.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: SKIN REDNESS AND IRRITATION
Description: Common side effect of external beam radiation therapy.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: MEMORY PROBLEMS
Description: Memory problems are long-term cognitive effects that may occur months or years after WBRT.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: CANCER TREATMENT
Description: CANCER TREATMENT is a comprehensive term encompassing the general category of therapy for cancer, as well as the overall process of treating specific cancers, such as ovarian cancer. It includes surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, systemic therapy, and other therapies, including clinical trials. The treatment plan is developed by the care team after the cancer has been staged and graded, and it is based on tumor location and cancer stage, with strategies often planned using test results. Cancer treatment is directed at the cancer itself, but it can have significant side effects: it can reactivate hepatitis viruses, affect fertility, and may require pregnancy prevention. Additionally, smoking or vaping can make cancer treatment less effective; therefore, treatment is more effective if the patient stops smoking or vaping. These considerations apply across the various forms of cancer therapy described.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: LOSS OF APPETITE
Description: Loss of appetite is a common side effect of radiation therapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: SLOWED THINKING
Description: Slowed thinking is a long-term cognitive effect that may occur months or years after WBRT.
Relationships: None listed
