    <node id="RECTAL CANCER">
      <data key="d0">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Rectal cancer is a malignant disease that forms in the rectum, which is the final section of the large intestine. As a cancer of the rectum, it is the focus of dedicated patient education materials: the NCCN Guidelines for Patients specifically address this disease and its treatment. These guidelines provide evidence-based information on managing rectal cancer, covering topics such as diagnosis, treatment options, and supportive care. The staging of rectal cancer ranges from stage I to stage III, and this classification is determined by two key factors: the extent of tumor growth into the rectal wall and the involvement of nearby lymph nodes. In summary, rectal cancer is a distinct cancer type with established clinical guidance for patients, and its staging system—based on local invasion and lymphatic spread—helps define the appropriate treatment approach.
Relationships:
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: COLORECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer is a colorectal cancer that forms in the rectum
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: NCCN
  - Description: NCCN, the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, publishes patient-focused resources on rectal cancer. Specifically, NCCN makes available the NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Rectal Cancer, which are designed to provide patients with reliable, evidence-based information about the disease and its management. These guidelines translate the clinical NCCN Guidelines into plain language so that patients and caregivers can better understand rectal cancer treatment options, decision-making, and care planning. The publication is part of NCCN's broader mission to improve the quality and effectiveness of cancer care by ensuring patients have access to clear, expert-derived guidance. In summary, the entity NCCN is the publisher, and its patient guideline dedicated to rectal cancer is a key resource available to affected individuals and their families.
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens are used to treat rectal cancer
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemoradiation is a possible next treatment for stage 2 and 3 rectal cancers
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: SURGICAL STAGING
  - Description: Surgical staging determines how far the rectal cancer has grown and whether lymph nodes contain cancer
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: PATHOLOGIST
  - Description: A pathologist assesses how far the rectal cancer has grown and whether removed lymph nodes contain cancer
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: LABORATORY TESTING
  - Description: Laboratory testing detects cancer in tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGE 1 RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer can be assigned stage 1 when the tumor has grown into the second or third muscle layer of the rectal wall
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGE 2 RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer can be assigned stage 2 when the tumor has grown through the rectal wall
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGE 3 RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer can be assigned stage 3 when cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Description: APR is used for tumors in the lower rectum caused by rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
