      <data key="d3">Colorectal cancer screening can detect colon cancer early, when it is most treatable
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: COLORECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Colon cancer is a colorectal cancer that forms in the colon
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: NCCN
  - Description: NCCN publishes the patient guide that discusses colon cancer diagnosis and treatment
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: POLYPS
  - Description: Almost all colon cancers start in a polyp, and removing polyps can prevent colon cancer
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: METASTATIC CANCER
  - Description: Metastatic cancer is a more advanced form of colon cancer that has spread
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: COLECTOMY
  - Description: Colectomy is needed for colon cancer not found early enough to be removed by polypectomy; it removes the cancerous part of the colon.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease's onset.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: ADENOMA
  - Description: Adenomas can become invasive colon cancer over time
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: COLONOSCOPY
  - Description: Colon cancer often forms in polyps, which are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon. Colonoscopy is the medical procedure used to view and remove these polyps, and it plays a central role in both the detection and diagnosis of colon cancer. Most colon cancers are found during a colonoscopy, as the procedure allows direct visualization of the colon and the ability to biopsy or remove suspicious tissue. In some cases, another colonoscopy may be needed to confirm a diagnosis, particularly when initial findings require further evaluation or when polyp removal was incomplete. Thus, colonoscopy serves as both a screening and diagnostic tool for colon cancer, linking polyp detection and removal to the broader process of diagnosing the disease.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Description: All colon cancers should be tested for mismatch repair mutations; mismatch repair testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: NON-METASTATIC CANCER
  - Description: Non-metastatic cancer is colon cancer that has not spread and is treated with surgery and sometimes chemotherapy
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: SURVIVORSHIP
  - Description: Survivorship focuses on long-term side effects and healthy habits after colon cancer treatment
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING
  - Description: Microsatellite instability testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY AFTER SURGERY
  - Description: The cancer stage determined after surgery helps determine whether chemotherapy after surgery is needed.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: CT with contrast is the main imaging test used to determine the extent of colon cancer.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: BLOOD TESTS
  - Description: Blood tests are recommended as part of initial testing for colon cancer.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Surveillance is part of the post-treatment management for colon cancer.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: FERTILITY AND FAMILY PLANNING
  - Description: Young adults diagnosed with colon cancer should be counseled about fertility risks and preservation options.</data>
