    <node id="TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY">
      <data key="d0">TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d1">TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Transabdominal surgery, also called abdominal surgery or transabdominal resection, is a surgical approach that involves cutting into the abdomen to remove a rectal tumor, surrounding healthy tissue, and nearby lymph nodes. After the procedure, the removed tissue and lymph nodes are sent to a laboratory for testing. This surgery is recommended for certain rectal cancer cases, including T2 tumors, some T1 tumors, and some larger T3 cancers that have not spread to lymph nodes. It is also performed after local excision when high-risk features or muscle invasion are identified. During transabdominal surgery, at least 12 lymph nodes should be removed for proper staging and evaluation.
Relationships:
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy may follow transabdominal resection
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Transabdominal resection/surgery is recommended if there are signs of cancer after chemoradiation
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: SURGICAL STAGING
  - Description: Tissue and lymph nodes removed during transabdominal surgery are sent to a lab and used for surgical staging
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: Some types of transabdominal surgery, such as LAR and APR, may require a colostomy
  - Source: TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Description: Transabdominal surgery is the preferred next treatment after local excision if high-risk features or a T2 tumor are found
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Abdominoperineal resection is a type of transabdominal surgery used for tumors in the lower rectum
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Description: APR is a transabdominal surgical procedure; after the surgery the removed tissue is sent for testing
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: LABORATORY TESTING
  - Description: After transabdominal surgery, the tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery are sent to a lab for testing
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Description: Low anterior resection is a type of transabdominal surgery used for tumors in the mid to upper rectum</data>
