    </node>
    <node id="TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION">
      <data key="d0">TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d1">TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Transanal local excision, also called local excision, is a surgery for T1 tumors at the end of the rectum. The surgeon cuts through all layers of the rectal wall to remove the cancer and some surrounding normal rectal tissue; lymph nodes are not removed.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)">
      <data key="d0">ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a radical surgical procedure performed through a transabdominal approach. It is used to treat persistent, progressive, or recurrent anal cancer, as well as tumors in the lower rectum that may have grown into the anus or pelvic floor muscle. The procedure involves the removal of the anus, the rectum, the area where the rectum and colon meet (rectosigmoid junction), and surrounding fat. Additional tissues may also be removed, including levator muscles and, in some cases, groin lymph nodes. APR always requires a permanent colostomy, as the natural passage for waste elimination is removed.
Relationships:
  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Both are types of transabdominal surgery for rectal tumors, but LAR is used for mid/upper rectum and APR for lower rectum</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)">
      <data key="d0">LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)</data>
      <data key="d1">LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Low anterior resection (LAR) is a type of transabdominal surgery used for tumors in the mid to upper rectum. It removes the rectal tumor and part or all of the sigmoid colon/last section of the colon. The colon may be reconnected to the remaining rectum (colorectal anastomosis) or attached to the anus (coloanal anastomosis). It allows for near-normal bowel movements and may require a temporary or permanent colostomy.
Relationships:
  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: COLORECTAL ANASTOMOSIS
  - Description: LAR may include colorectal anastomosis when the colon is reconnected to the remaining rectum
  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: COLOANAL ANASTOMOSIS
  - Description: LAR may include coloanal anastomosis when the colon is attached directly to the anus
  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: COLOSTOMY
  - Description: LAR may require a colostomy if bowel reattachment is delayed or impossible; it may be temporary or permanent
  - Source: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Target: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
  - Description: Both are types of transabdominal surgery for rectal tumors, but LAR is used for mid/upper rectum and APR for lower rectum
  - Source: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
  - Target: LOW ANTERIOR RESECTION (LAR)
  - Description: Low anterior resection is a type of transabdominal surgery used for tumors in the mid to upper rectum</data>
    </node>
