    <node id="ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER">
      <data key="d0">ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Advanced-stage prostate cancer is prostate cancer that has spread outside the prostate to other areas of the body, including lymph nodes, bones, liver, lungs, or other organs. It is also referred to as metastatic or distant metastatic prostate cancer. This condition encompasses both regional prostate cancer and metastatic prostate cancer. Advanced-stage prostate cancer is usually a lifelong disease; however, treatment can slow its progression and help manage the condition.
Relationships:
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: Advanced-stage prostate cancer involves spread of cancer cells; this spreading is called metastasis.
  - Source: ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Advanced-stage prostate cancer is defined by metastasis, meaning the cancer has spread beyond the prostate to other parts of the body. This condition is characterized by the movement of cancerous cells from the prostate gland to distant sites, such as bones, lymph nodes, or other organs. In the context of this disease, metastasis is the key feature that distinguishes advanced-stage prostate cancer from localized prostate cancer, which remains confined within the prostate. Thus, advanced-stage prostate cancer and metastasis are closely linked, with the latter serving as the defining criterion for the former.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
