    <node id="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER">
      <data key="d0">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is a medical organization that created the widely used cancer staging system to describe the extent of cancer in the body. Its system is designed to determine how much cancer is present, where it is located, and what subtype it is, thereby guiding prognosis and treatment decisions. The AJCC developed the TNM staging system, which evaluates three key components: tumor size and extent (T), lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M). In addition to TNM scores, the AJCC staging system incorporates tumor grade and, for certain cancers, biomarker testing—such as hormone receptor and HER2 status in breast cancer—to refine prognosis and guide therapy.

The AJCC staging system includes both clinical and pathologic staging and assigns numbered stages (e.g., stage I, II, III, IV) that represent the overall cancer burden. These numbered stages are used across multiple cancer types. For example, in mouth cancer, the AJCC system defines TNM and numbered stages; in colon cancer, it combines T, N, and M scores to stage the disease; in anal cancer and perianal tumors, it uses the TNM framework; and it also developed one of the staging systems for ovarian cancer, known as the AJCC staging system. In breast cancer, the AJCC system uses tumor size, lymph node involvement, metastasis, tumor grade, and hormone receptor/HER2 status to predict outcomes and inform treatment.

Overall, the American Joint Committee on Cancer serves as the authoritative organization for cancer staging, providing a standardized method for clinicians to describe cancer extent, location, and subtype, and to communicate prognosis across different malignancies. Its staging framework is integral to cancer diagnosis, treatment planning, and research, ensuring consistent classification for a broad range of cancers.

**Relationships:**
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) develops patient guidelines that reference, describe, and apply the cancer staging system created by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC). In its patient education materials, the NCCN uses the AJCC staging system as the foundational framework for explaining how cancer is staged. For instance, NCCN’s mouth cancer staging patient information describes and explains the AJCC staging system in detail. Similarly, NCCN’s patient guidelines on throat cancer staging explain and apply the same AJCC staging system, with a dedicated chapter on throat cancer staging that relies on the AJCC framework. Across these various cancer-specific resources, the NCCN consistently incorporates the AJCC staging system to help patients understand the extent of their disease, prognosis, and treatment planning. Thus, the NCCN’s patient-facing guidelines are aligned with the authoritative staging definitions and criteria established by the American Joint Committee on Cancer, ensuring that patients receive standardized, evidence-based information about cancer staging.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: The AJCC TNM system assesses M (metastasis) to stage colon cancer.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created and developed the cancer staging system described in the text, which is the staging system used for cancer staging. This AJCC staging system is one of the two standard ways to describe the extent of ovarian cancer. As the entity responsible for this widely used framework, the American Joint Committee on Cancer established a systematic method for categorizing the progression and spread of cancer, providing clinicians and researchers with a common language to describe tumor characteristics. The cancer staging system developed by the committee is integral to treatment planning and prognosis, and its application to ovarian cancer represents one of its key clinical uses, where it serves as a standard alongside another classification approach to define how far the disease has advanced.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: STAGING
  - Description: The AJCC TNM system is used to stage colon cancer, assigning stages based on T, N, and M scores.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: PATHOLOGIC STAGING
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed one of the staging systems used for pathologic staging of ovarian cancer.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) and the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) have both developed staging systems for ovarian cancer. The two systems are similar in structure and intent, providing standardized frameworks for classifying the extent of the disease. Although both staging systems are recognized and used in clinical practice, the FIGO system is employed most often. This reflects its widespread acceptance and practical utility in the management and communication of ovarian cancer cases.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: TNM SCORES
  - Description: The AJCC staging system uses TNM scores to assign a cancer stage.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: NPC STAGING
  - Description: The AJCC staging system is used in NPC staging.

  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: TNM SCORING
  - Description: The AJCC staging system uses TNM scoring to describe the extent of cancer.

---</data>
    </node>
