    <node id="PATHOLOGIC STAGING">
      <data key="d0">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Pathologic staging, also referred to as surgical staging, is the post-surgical process of determining the extent to which cancer has spread. It is based on laboratory findings from tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery. The stage is assigned by a pathologist who examines the surgically removed specimens, such as a lymph node, to rate the cancer. This staging method is also known as the surgical stage and is designated with categories such as pN (pathologic node stage). Pathologic staging provides more precise information about the cancer than clinical staging because it is derived from direct examination of actual tissue. It is commonly used for various cancers, including ovarian cancer, where the post-surgery determination of how far the disease has spread relies on information gained during surgery and surgical staging.

**Relationships:**
  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: PATHOLOGIC STAGING
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed one of the staging systems used for pathologic staging of ovarian cancer.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: PATHOLOGIC STAGING
Description: Pathologic staging, also referred to as surgical staging, is the post-surgical process of determining the extent to which cancer has spread. It is based on laboratory findings from tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery. The stage is assigned by a pathologist who examines the surgically removed specimens, such as a lymph node, to rate the cancer. This staging method is also known as the surgical stage and is designated with categories such as pN (pathologic node stage). Pathologic staging provides more precise information about the cancer than clinical staging because it is derived from direct examination of actual tissue. It is commonly used for various cancers, including ovarian cancer, where the post-surgery determination of how far the disease has spread relies on information gained during surgery and surgical staging.

**Relationships:**
  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: PATHOLOGIC STAGING
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed one of the staging systems used for pathologic staging of ovarian cancer.

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHILE THE OVERARCHING CONCEPT OF STAGING IS CONSISTENT—DEFINING DISEASE EXTENT AND SPREAD—THE OPERATIONAL DETAILS, INCLUDING STAGING SYSTEMS AND SPECIFIC STAGE DEFINITIONS, ARE TAILORED TO EACH CANCER TYPE. THUS, STAGING SERVES AS A UNIVERSAL ONCOLOGIC TOOL THAT IS IMPLEMENTED THROUGH CANCER-SPECIFIC CLASSIFICATION SYSTEMS, SUCH AS FIGO FOR GYNECOLOGIC CANCERS AND AJCC TNM FOR COLON AND MANY OTHER SOLID TUMORS. THIS ENSURES THAT CLINICIANS AND PATIENTS HAVE A STANDARDIZED, EVIDENCE-BASED LANGUAGE FOR DESCRIBING CANCER PROGRESSION, FACILITATING TREATMENT PLANNING AND COMMUNICATION ACROSS HEALTHCARE TEAMS. IN SUMMARY, STAGING IS NOT A SINGLE UNIFORM PROCEDURE BUT A FRAMEWORK ADAPTED TO THE ANATOMY AND BEHAVIOR OF EACH CANCER, WITH ALL APPROACHES ULTIMATELY AIMING TO QUANTIFY TUMOR BURDEN AND SPREAD.">
