    <node id="DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM">
      <data key="d0">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d1">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A diagnostic mammogram is an X-ray imaging test of the breast that takes detailed pictures of both breasts to detect abnormalities, such as cancer or other changes. It is distinct from a screening mammogram, which is used for routine detection in people without symptoms. A diagnostic mammogram is specifically used for individuals who have symptoms such as a lump, breast pain, or nipple changes, or to take a closer look at an abnormal area that was found on a screening mammogram. During this procedure, a radiologist evaluates the images immediately, allowing for prompt assessment of the findings. This test serves as a follow-up and diagnostic tool to clarify whether a suspicious area is benign or requires further investigation.
Relationships:
  - Source: MAMMOGRAM
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: A mammogram is an imaging examination used to examine breast tissue. A diagnostic mammogram is a specific type of mammogram performed when there are symptoms or abnormal findings that require further evaluation. Specifically, diagnostic mammograms are used to assess symptoms such as a lump, pain, or nipple discharge, or to follow up on abnormal results from a routine screening mammogram. In essence, while a screening mammogram is used for early detection in asymptomatic individuals, a diagnostic mammogram is a focused examination intended to clarify and evaluate suspicious signs or prior screening abnormalities, providing more detailed imaging to aid in diagnosis.
  - Source: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: A screening mammogram is an initial imaging exam used to detect potential breast abnormalities in individuals without symptoms. When a screening mammogram reveals an abnormal area, a diagnostic mammogram is performed as a follow-up. A diagnostic mammogram is used to take a closer look at the abnormal area found on the screening mammogram, providing more detailed images to help determine whether the finding is benign or requires further investigation. Together, the screening mammogram serves as the first-line detection tool, while the diagnostic mammogram provides focused evaluation of any suspicious findings identified during screening.
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Target: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: A diagnostic mammogram is different from a screening mammogram
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: Diagnostic mammograms are used when symptoms such as a lump, pain, or nipple changes may indicate breast cancer.
  - Source: RADIOLOGIST
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: A radiologist evaluates the diagnostic mammogram while the patient waits
  - Source: BREAST CANCER STAGING
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: Diagnostic mammograms provide imaging information used in breast cancer staging
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: Diagnostic mammogram is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
