    <node id="MELANOMA">
      <data key="d0">MELANOMA</data>
      <data key="d1">MELANOMA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Melanoma is a type of cancer that most commonly arises as a skin cancer, though it can also develop in other mucosal surfaces, such as the anus, where it is known as anal melanoma, a rare form of anal cancer. The entity is referenced in text that directs readers to the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which provide detailed, patient-focused information on this disease. These guidelines are available specifically for melanoma and cover aspects of diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care. In summary, melanoma is a serious cancer, with skin being its primary site, and anal melanoma representing a rare variant; authoritative patient guidance is accessible through the NCCN Guidelines for Patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: MELANOMA
  - Description: The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides patient-focused guidelines specifically addressing melanoma. These NCCN Guidelines for Patients are designed to offer accessible information about the disease, its management, and related care. In addition, the source material directs readers to these NCCN Guidelines for Patients as a recommended resource for further details on melanoma, reinforcing their role as a key reference for patient education and guidance.
  - Source: MELANOMA
  - Target: BASAL CELL SKIN CANCER
  - Description: Both are types of skin cancer; the text contrasts basal cell skin cancer with melanoma and directs readers to NCCN for more information
  - Source: SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER
  - Target: MELANOMA
  - Description: Both are types of skin cancer mentioned together as topics covered by NCCN patient materials
  - Source: MELANOMA
  - Target: SKIN CANCER
  - Description: Melanoma is mentioned as a type of skin cancer

---</data>
