  - Description: Heart tests may be used to monitor treatment side effects. 

---

**Note:** The entity "SIDE EFFECTS" itself has a description provided in the data: "SIDE EFFECTS are unintended effects that can arise from medical treatment, most notably external beam radiation therapy (EBRT). Common adverse effects of EBRT include fatigue, skin redness and irritation, diarrhea, nausea, and frequent or painful urination. These effects may be monitored during treatment—for example, heart tests can be used to track treatment-related side effects—and supportive care is often provided to address and manage them. Together, these descriptions highlight that side effects are both a known set of physical reactions to radiation therapy and a broader concept encompassing any unintended consequences of treatment that require monitoring and supportive intervention." 

Additionally, the entity "SURVIVORSHIP CARE FOR CANCER-RELATED LATE AND LONG-TERM EFFECTS" is related to the NCCN (which publishes it) but is not directly connected to "SIDE EFFECTS" in the provided relationships, so it is not included above.</data>
