    <node id="DENTAL EXAM">
      <data key="d0">DENTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d1">DENTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A dental exam is recommended prior to undergoing head and neck cancer treatment, as such treatment can significantly affect the health of the jaw, teeth, and gums. This dental evaluation is an important preparatory step, often including imaging such as an x-ray (Panorex) or a CT scan without contrast, to assess the condition of the oral structures. The examination is essential because head and neck cancer treatment—such as radiation therapy or surgery—can have direct impacts on the jaw, potentially leading to complications like osteoradionecrosis or other dental and periodontal issues. By identifying existing dental problems and planning necessary interventions beforehand, the dental exam helps optimize oral health and minimize treatment-related risks, ensuring better overall outcomes for the patient.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: DENTAL EXAM
  - Description: Head and neck cancer treatment can significantly affect the jaw, teeth, and gums, making dental health a critical part of patient care. Because of these potential impacts, a dental exam is highly important for individuals undergoing or preparing for treatment. Regular dental cleanings and checkups are essential to monitor and maintain oral health, as head and neck cancer therapies—such as radiation or surgery—can compromise the structures of the mouth and jaw. Dental exams help identify and manage complications early, reducing the risk of serious dental issues during and after cancer treatment. Thus, for head and neck cancer patients, consistent dental care is a vital component of overall treatment and long-term wellbeing.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: DENTAL EXAM
Description: A dental exam is recommended prior to undergoing head and neck cancer treatment, as such treatment can significantly affect the health of the jaw, teeth, and gums. This dental evaluation is an important preparatory step, often including imaging such as an x-ray (Panorex) or a CT scan without contrast, to assess the condition of the oral structures. The examination is essential because head and neck cancer treatment—such as radiation therapy or surgery—can have direct impacts on the jaw, potentially leading to complications like osteoradionecrosis or other dental and periodontal issues. By identifying existing dental problems and planning necessary interventions beforehand, the dental exam helps optimize oral health and minimize treatment-related risks, ensuring better overall outcomes for the patient.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: DENTAL EXAM
  - Description: Head and neck cancer treatment can significantly affect the jaw, teeth, and gums, making dental health a critical part of patient care. Because of these potential impacts, a dental exam is highly important for individuals undergoing or preparing for treatment. Regular dental cleanings and checkups are essential to monitor and maintain oral health, as head and neck cancer therapies—such as radiation or surgery—can compromise the structures of the mouth and jaw. Dental exams help identify and manage complications early, reducing the risk of serious dental issues during and after cancer treatment. Thus, for head and neck cancer patients, consistent dental care is a vital component of overall treatment and long-term wellbeing.</data>
