      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN Cancer Centers serve as a key resource for identifying and accessing clinical trials. These centers are formally recognized as a U.S.-based resource for individuals seeking information about clinical trial opportunities. The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Cancer Centers provide a curated listing that helps patients, caregivers, and healthcare professionals locate relevant clinical trials across the United States. By utilizing these centers, users can connect with ongoing research studies and potentially participate in the advancement of cancer treatment. The entity "NCCN CANCER CENTERS" is thus directly associated with the facilitation of "CLINICAL TRIAL" discovery. Together, the descriptions emphasize that NCCN Cancer Centers are a reliable, U.S.-specific source for finding clinical trials, offering a centralized approach to navigating the complex landscape of cancer research.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN Cancer Centers is listed as a resource for finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="UNITED STATES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
      <data key="d8">UNITED STATES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment and research institutions located throughout the United States. These centers are recognized as a key resource for patients and providers seeking information about clinical trials. Specifically, the NCCN Cancer Centers are listed under the category "In the United States" as a resource for finding clinical trials. This listing indicates that the centers serve as a designated point of reference for individuals looking to identify and access clinical trial opportunities within the country. The descriptions consistently emphasize that NCCN Cancer Centers are based in the United States and function as a practical tool for clinical trial discovery, making the information accessible and relevant to a U.S. audience. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers comprise a U.S.-based network, and they are formally indexed as a clinical trial finding resource within the United States, ensuring that patients and clinicians can readily locate trials through these centers.</data>
      <data key="d12">UNITED STATES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) and NCCN Cancer Centers are both U.S. organizations listed as resources for finding cancer clinical trials. They serve as authoritative sources for locating clinical trials available in the United States. NCCN Cancer Centers can be accessed online at NCCN.org/cancercenters, while the NCIs corresponding resource is available at cancer.gov. Both are recognized for providing information on cancer clinical trials to patients, caregivers, and researchers.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN Cancer Centers and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both recognized resources for locating clinical trials. NCCN Cancer Centers are specifically listed as a resource for finding clinical trials within the United States, while the NLM provides the worldwide clinicaltrials.gov database. Together, these two entities offer complementary access to clinical trial information: NCCN Cancer Centers focus on U.S.-based cancer centers, and the NLM supplies a comprehensive, global database of clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Primary CNS lymphoma can be treated to slow growth, reduce symptoms, and prolong life</data>
      <data key="d12">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team is central to the entire cancer treatment process, particularly for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Their responsibilities begin with planning the next steps of care, which are determined based on imaging and test results. Using these findings, the care team identifies the appropriate course of action and outlines the treatment pathway. The care team provides treatment options and comprehensive care to the patient. Treatment planning is a collaborative process: the patient and care team together choose a treatment plan that aligns with the patients needs and preferences. Once a plan is agreed upon, the care team coordinates care throughout the entire treatment journey, ensuring that all aspects of the plan are implemented smoothly. In addition to planning, the care team actively prescribes and monitors treatments. They ensure that each treatment is properly initiated, and they list the start date of each treatment. This monitoring is essential for tracking the patients response and adjusting the plan as needed. Ultimately, the care team provides the planned cancer treatment itself, delivering the therapies that have been selected for the patient. Through this combination of planning, prescribing, monitoring, coordinating, and providing care, the care team manages the full continuum of AML treatment, supporting the patient from initial decision-making through ongoing therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical trials are a type of treatment option, specifically one that may be recommended for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). As a treatment approach, clinical trials offer eligible individuals access to investigational therapies and contribute to the advancement of medical knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiationa combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Treatment is evaluated in metastatic cancer, and targeted therapy may be used for certain metastatic cases.</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
