      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Treatments can cause side effects and side effects are a key part of treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d12">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Radiation therapy is a type of treatment that falls under the broader category of medical treatments. Specifically, it is a type of cancer treatment, used to address malignant diseases. Additionally, radiation therapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer, indicating its application in managing that particular form of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A health care provider gives information about next steps and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Hematopoietic cell transplant is a type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). It is categorized under the broader entity of TREATMENT, specifically serving as a therapeutic option for AML. As a treatment modality, hematopoietic cell transplant involves the infusion of healthy blood-forming stem cells to replace damaged or diseased bone marrow, and it is used in certain cases of AML to help restore normal blood cell production. This description confirms that hematopoietic cell transplant is recognized as an AML treatment, aligning with standard medical practice where it may be employed in specific patient populations depending on disease risk and other clinical factors.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer treatment is a specific type of treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RECURRENCE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Treatment and recurrence are closely linked concepts in cancer care. After a patient completes treatment, follow-up imaging is commonly used to monitor for any signs that the cancer has returned, a process known as recurrence. This surveillance helps detect whether the cancer has come back after initial therapy. Additionally, risk assessment plays a key role in evaluating the likelihood of recurrence following treatment, as well as determining whether a recurrence can be effectively managed with an alternative treatment approach. Together, these descriptions highlight that treatment is not only aimed at eliminating cancer but also involves ongoing monitoring and risk evaluation to address the possibility of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d12">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
