    <edge source="NCCN" target="SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d8">SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides patient-friendly guidelines for squamous cell skin cancer. These guidelines are accessible through the NCCN website at NCCN.org/patientguidelines and through the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer mobile application. The materials direct readers to the NCCN Guidelines for Patients as a comprehensive source of information on squamous cell skin cancer, offering guidance on diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care. The NCCN thus serves as a key resource for patients seeking reliable, expert-reviewed information about this condition.</data>
      <data key="d12">SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients list the U.S. National Library of Medicine as a resource.</data>
      <data key="d12">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both recognized resources for locating clinical trials. NCCN provides clinical trial resources specifically for the United States, primarily through its network of NCCN Cancer Centers. In contrast, the U.S. National Library of Medicine offers worldwide clinical trial resources, most notably through the ClinicalTrials.gov database. Together, they serve as complementary tools: NCCN focuses on U.S.-based trial options, while NLM covers global trial listings.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
