    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team commonly requests a biopsy sample to confirm a diagnosis of glioma. In addition, the care team uses the results from that biopsy to help determine the patient's cancer risk. Together, the biopsy and the care team are closely connected: the biopsy provides essential diagnostic information, while the care team relies on this information both to confirm the presence of glioma and to assess the overall risk associated with the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="COMPLICATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">COMPLICATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Having a biopsy may cause complications such as infection, bleeding, or blood in the urine, stool, or semen.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If chemotherapy is planned first, a biopsy is performed to remove a sample of the tumor before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy is a medical procedure in which a sample of tissue is taken from the body for examination, and it is required to diagnose ovarian cancer. This procedure is performed not only to confirm the presence of ovarian cancer but also to determine its specific type and grade, which are essential for guiding treatment decisions. Thus, the biopsy serves as the definitive diagnostic tool for ovarian cancer, providing critical information about the disease's characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy samples are examined to look for cancer cells</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="STEREOTACTIC BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">STEREOTACTIC BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Stereotactic biopsy is one of the standard types of biopsy for gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy and surgery are two closely related medical procedures that both involve the removal of biological material for pathological examination. In both biopsy and surgery, samples of tissue are collected and sent to a pathology laboratory for analysis. Pathologists are the specialists responsible for examining the cells, tissues, and organs removed during either a biopsy or a surgical procedure, providing crucial diagnostic information. Biopsy is typically performed as part of the initial surgical intervention to remove cancer. When a patient undergoes surgery for cancer, the tumor and other affected tissues removed during the operation are routinely tested, and this removed tissue effectively serves as biopsy material for pathological evaluation. In this way, surgery can provide the necessary biopsy specimens even when a separate, dedicated biopsy procedure was not performed beforehand. Additionally, the results from any prior surgery and earlier biopsy procedures are often reviewed to inform treatment decisions and to compare with current findings. The relationship between the two procedures is complementary: a biopsy can be a standalone, minimally invasive procedure used to obtain a small sample for diagnosis, or it can be integrated into a larger surgical operation where the entire tumor or organ is removed and subsequently examined. When surgery is planned as the first step, the tissues excised during the operation are submitted for testing, fulfilling the role of a biopsy. Conversely, when a biopsy is performed first, its results help guide the surgical plan. Ultimately, both biopsy and surgery are essential sources of tissue specimens, and the pathologist's analysis of these specimens forms the foundation for accurate diagnosis, staging, and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scan may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ultrasound plays a key role in biopsy procedures, serving both as a guidance tool and as a diagnostic aid. During a biopsy, ultrasound can be used to guide the needle to the precise location of a target tissue, improving accuracy and safety. This guidance is especially useful for lymph node biopsies, where ultrasound helps direct the procedure to the affected node. Additionally, ultrasound results help determine whether a needle biopsy is needed for a thyroid nodule, as imaging findings can indicate whether sampling is necessary. Thus, ultrasound supports biopsy decision-making and execution across different clinical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OPEN BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">OPEN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Open biopsy is a type of biopsy, which is a medical procedure used to obtain tissue samples for diagnosis. Specifically, open biopsy is recognized as one of the standard types of biopsy for gliomas, a form of brain tumor. Thus, within the broader category of biopsy, open biopsy holds particular importance in the diagnostic evaluation of gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy results and biopsy findings play a central role in guiding cancer treatment decisions. After a cancer diagnosis, these results are used to help plan the appropriate treatment approach. Additionally, biopsy findings provide specific information about which treatments the cancer may respond to, enabling more targeted and effective therapy. Together, biopsy and treatment are directly connected: the biopsy supplies essential diagnostic and predictive data, while treatment planning relies on that data to determine the best course of action for each patient.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PATIENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The patient undergoes a biopsy to evaluate for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy and biomarker testing are closely related procedures in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer. A biopsy involves obtaining a sample of tumor tissue from the patient. This biopsy sample is then used for biomarker testing, which analyzes the tumor for specific molecular components, such as genes, proteins, or other biomarkers. In other words, biomarker testing relies on a tissue sample obtained through biopsy, allowing healthcare providers to identify molecular characteristics of the tumor that may guide treatment decisions. Together, biopsy and biomarker testing enable a detailed understanding of the tumor's biology.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy can be taken from a metastasis, for example in the liver, using a hollow needle.</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
