    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="FDG-PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG-PET/CT is a specialized imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with computed tomography (CT). As a type of PET/CT scan, it inherently includes a PET scan component, which is integrated with CT to provide both functional and anatomical information. The technique utilizes a radiotracerspecifically fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)to detect glucose uptake, which is particularly useful for identifying cancer cells due to their heightened metabolic activity. In summary, FDG-PET/CT is a PET scan combined with CT that leverages FDG to visualize glucose metabolism, making it a valuable tool in oncology and other medical applications.</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="PET/CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A PET/CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that combines a PET scan with CT imaging. The term "PET/CT scan" refers to the integration of positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single examination, allowing for both functional and anatomical information to be captured in one session. This combined approach is also described as a PET scan combined with a CT scan. The PET/CT scan is therefore a fusion of PET and CT technologies, providing a comprehensive diagnostic tool. The entity "PET SCAN" is a component of the "PET/CT SCAN," which specifically refers to the combined imaging modality.</data>
      <data key="d12">PET/CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
