    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET scans may be combined with MRI for imaging.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="MRI SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI SCAN (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) and PET SCAN (Positron Emission Tomography) are two distinct imaging methods that can be combined into a single PET/MRI scanning procedure. In this combined approach, the PET scan and MRI scan work together to provide a more detailed and comprehensive image than either modality could offer alone. The PET component is particularly useful for identifying areas of abnormal metabolic activity, which helps determine where cancer cells are located. Meanwhile, the MRI component supplies high-resolution anatomical detail of soft tissues. By integrating both technologies, PET/MRI scanning enables clinicians to correlate functional information from the PET scan with structural information from the MRI scan, leading to more accurate diagnosis and localization of disease. Thus, MRI SCAN and PET SCAN are complementary imaging tools, and their combined use in PET/MRI scanning enhances the ability to pinpoint cancerous sites and assess the extent of disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="BONE SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">BONE SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Both bone scan and PET scan are nuclear medicine imaging procedures that use radioactive tracers to detect cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">BONE SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET SCAN" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT SCAN and PET SCAN are two distinct medical imaging methods that are frequently used together to improve diagnostic accuracy. A PET (positron emission tomography) scan and a CT (computed tomography) scan are often combined into a single procedure known as a PET/CT scan. This combined approach is specifically used to determine where cancer cells are located in the body, and it provides a more detailed image than either scan could offer alone. The PET scanner itself is a machine similar in appearance and function to an MRI or CT scanner. When the two modalities are integrated, the resulting PET/CT scan is a standard and powerful imaging technique that helps clinicians pinpoint disease activity and anatomical location simultaneously.</data>
      <data key="d12">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
