    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Diagnostic mammograms are used when symptoms such as a lump, pain, or nipple changes may indicate breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) is a form of breast cancer, specifically classified as a noninvasive, stage 0 condition. As a breast cancer subtype, DCIS is characterized by the presence of abnormal cells that are confined within the milk ducts of the breast and have not spread into surrounding breast tissue. This noninvasive nature distinguishes it from invasive breast cancers, and it is considered the earliest stage of breast cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d12">DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU</data>
    </edge>
