      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The HPV vaccine prevents new HPV infections but does not treat existing infections</data>
      <data key="d12">HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS INFECTION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TUMOR HPV TESTING" target="HPV-RELATED CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TUMOR HPV TESTING</data>
      <data key="d8">HPV-RELATED CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Tumor HPV testing can identify whether a cancer is HPV-related.</data>
      <data key="d12">HPV-RELATED CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="INFANT ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">INFANT ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical guidance through its NCCN Guidelines, which include dedicated content addressing infant acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients contain a chapter that discusses infant ALL, and the broader NCCN Guidelines also include a chapter focused on infant ALL treatment. These materials are part of the organizations efforts to offer evidence-based recommendations and patient-oriented information for this rare and challenging form of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">INFANT ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) publishes patient care guidelines specifically for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). These NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide comprehensive information about ALL, including details on testing, treatment options, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical guidance that includes a dedicated chapter on the treatment of BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL. This information is presented in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which specifically address management and treatment options for this subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical practice guidelines that address the treatment of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines include a dedicated chapter on B-ALL treatment, and the NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter covering this topic. These resources are designed to help healthcare professionals and patients understand evidence-based approaches to managing B-ALL, from diagnosis through treatment planning. The NCCN thus offers comprehensive, standardized guidance on B-ALL, ensuring that both clinical practitioners and patients have access to current, expert-reviewed information on therapeutic options for this disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines that include a chapter on BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL, which is identified as the most common type of B-ALL. The NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter specifically addressing treatment for BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL, offering patient-focused information on managing this condition.</data>
      <data key="d12">BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="T-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a professional organization that develops evidence-based guidelines for cancer care. Its NCCN Guidelines include a chapter dedicated to T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). This chapter appears in both the patient-focused version of the guidelines, where it discusses T-ALL in an accessible manner, and in the clinical guidelines, where it provides detailed treatment recommendations. Thus, the NCCN Guidelines serve as a resource for understanding T-ALL and its management, covering both educational and therapeutic aspects of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Guidelines mention non-Hodgkin lymphoma when explaining Burkitt lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d12">NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN publishes patient guidelines that cover diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care for ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="INFANT ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">INFANT ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Infant ALL is ALL in infants under 12 months of age.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="NCCN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources for people seeking ALL treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">ALL is the acronym for acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Burkitt leukemia, also known as mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), is a rare subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia. It is a distinct form of the disease, categorized under the broader entity of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and is recognized for its mature B-cell phenotype.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="T-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts. One of the two main types of ALL is T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). T-ALL specifically originates in T-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell that plays a central role in the immune system. As one of the two primary subtypes of ALL, T-ALL is distinguished from the other main type, which arises from B-cell lymphocytes. Together, these two subtypes encompass the majority of acute lymphoblastic leukemia cases. T-ALL develops when genetic mutations or other alterations cause T-cell precursors to multiply uncontrollably, leading to an accumulation of abnormal cells in the bone marrow, blood, and sometimes other organs. The disease is aggressive and requires prompt treatment, typically involving chemotherapy, and in some cases targeted therapy or stem cell transplantation. Although T-ALL and B-ALL share many clinical features, they differ in their cellular origins, immunophenotypic markers, and certain molecular characteristics, which can influence treatment response and prognosis. T-ALL is more common in adolescents and young adults, while B-ALL is more frequent in younger children. Because T-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, all general information about ALLincluding its causes, symptoms such as fatigue, fever, easy bruising, and bone pain, as well as diagnostic approaches like blood counts and bone marrow biopsyalso applies to T-ALL. However, specific management strategies for T-ALL may be adapted based on the distinct biology of T-cell disease. In summary, T-ALL is one of the two main forms of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, originating specifically from T-cell lymphocytes, and is a distinct yet closely related entity within the broader ALL classification.</data>
      <data key="d12">T-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">T-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="ADOLESCENT AND YOUNG ADULT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">ADOLESCENT AND YOUNG ADULT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">This book applies to AYAs and adults being treated for ALL; AYAs are a unique patient group diagnosed with ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
