    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="ANAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team talks with the patient about the types of chemotherapy, doses, timing, and side effects</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The multidisciplinary team coordinates chemotherapy as part of the treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d12">MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy is needed to confirm ovarian cancer before chemotherapy is started</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapytermed chemoradiationrepresents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy uses anti-cancer medicines that travel in the bloodstream to reach cells throughout the body, including cancer cells</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The clinical trial could involve treatment with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Metastatic anal cancer is treated with platinum-based chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells. Chemoradiation is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. In this combined regimen, chemotherapy serves as one of the core components, specifically functioning as the chemotherapy portion of the treatment. Chemoradiation may be followed by additional chemotherapy, which is given to kill any lingering cancer cells that remain after the initial combined treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
