    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The treatment team considers many factors to decide if a stem cell rescue is right for a patient</data>
      <data key="d12">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
      <data key="d8">HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure used in the treatment of lymphoma, and high-dose chemotherapy is a critical component of this process. High-dose chemotherapy is administered as a key step in stem cell rescue, specifically designed to kill lymphoma cells before the replacement of stem cells takes place. It serves as the second, cell-destroying step of the overall stem cell rescue procedure, highlighting its role in eradicating malignant cells to prepare the body for new, healthy stem cells. Together, these entities form a treatment strategy in which high-dose chemotherapy clears the way for stem cell replacement, making it an essential part of the rescue process.</data>
      <data key="d12">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
      <data key="d8">LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Stem cell rescue is used when lymphoma comes back after earlier treatment or after other treatments</data>
      <data key="d12">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="STEM CELL REMOVAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
      <data key="d8">STEM CELL REMOVAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Stem cell removal is the first step of a stem cell rescue</data>
      <data key="d12">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="STEM CELL REPLACEMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
      <data key="d8">STEM CELL REPLACEMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Stem cell replacement is the final step of a stem cell rescue</data>
      <data key="d12">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="RELAPSE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
      <data key="d8">RELAPSE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Stem cell rescue is a treatment approach used for lymphoma when the disease has returned after an initial course of therapy. Specifically, it is indicated in the context of a relapse, meaning the lymphoma has come back following earlier treatment. The procedure is employed after the relapse occurs, serving as a subsequent line of intervention. Thus, stem cell rescue is directly associated with managing recurrent lymphoma, with the goal of addressing the disease after prior treatment has failed to provide a lasting remission.</data>
      <data key="d12">STEM CELL RESCUE</data>
    </edge>
