    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="ANOSCOPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">ANOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Both DREs and anoscopy are follow-up tests recommended after treatment for cancer in lymph nodes only</data>
      <data key="d12">ANOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="PSA TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A PSA test (prostate-specific antigen test) and a digital rectal exam are two initial screening procedures commonly used together to evaluate prostate health and assess the possible presence of prostate cancer. The PSA test measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, while the digital rectal exam involves a physical examination of the prostate gland. When used in combination, these tests help healthcare providers determine whether further diagnostic steps, such as a prostate biopsy, are needed. Their combined use provides a more complete evaluation than either test alone, aiding in the early detection and informed decision-making regarding prostate conditions.</data>
      <data key="d12">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If a digital rectal exam suggests prostate cancer, diagnostic testing may be offered.</data>
      <data key="d12">DIAGNOSTIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A digital rectal exam (DRE) is a clinical procedure used to assess the prostate gland. When an irregular or hardened area is detected during this exam, it serves as a key indicator for further investigation. The results of the digital rectal exam directly inform the clinical decision of whether a prostate biopsy is necessary. Thus, the DRE acts as an initial screening tool, and any suspicious findingssuch as firmness or irregularitiesprompt the consideration of a biopsy to obtain definitive tissue diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A digital rectal exam is used along with imaging and PSA tests to evaluate the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A digital rectal exam (DRE) is a clinical procedure used to assess the prostate gland, particularly in the context of prostate cancer. During the exam, a healthcare provider directly checks the prostate for signs of cancer, evaluating its size and texture. The information obtained contributes to the overall assessment of prostate cancer risk and helps guide further diagnostic steps. Thus, the digital rectal exam serves as a key component in the detection and evaluation of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Your doctor performs the digital rectal exam.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" target="TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d8">TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A digital rectal exam is one of the common prostate tests used during testing.</data>
      <data key="d12">TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="ANAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer and is used for anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="ANAL CANAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemoradiation is a possible next treatment for stage 2 and 3 rectal cancers</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMORADIATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemoradiation is a cancer treatment modality that integrates two therapeutic approaches: chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Specifically, it involves the administration of radiation therapy concurrently with chemotherapy, meaning the two treatments are given together during the same treatment period rather than sequentially. This combination is designed to enhance the overall effectiveness of cancer treatment, as the chemotherapy can sensitize tumor cells to the radiation, while the radiation therapy targets the localized tumor area. Thus, chemoradiation is defined as a treatment that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy, with the key feature being the concurrent delivery of both therapies. Radiation therapy, on its own, uses high-energy radiation to destroy or shrink tumors, and when combined with chemotherapy in this manner, it forms the integrated treatment strategy known as chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMORADIATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Digital rectal examination is recommended 8 to 12 weeks after completing chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d12">DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="COMPLETE REMISSION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">COMPLETE REMISSION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Complete remission is a possible outcome that is evaluated after chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d12">COMPLETE REMISSION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">This regimen is one of the chemotherapy regimens used as part of chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d12">MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="DRE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">DRE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A follow-up DRE is recommended 8 to 12 weeks after completing chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d12">DRE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="LOCAL EXCISION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Local excision may be used as an alternative to chemoradiation for some anal cancers. Specifically, it can be considered instead of chemoradiation for certain slow-growing stage I and II perianal cancers. Thus, for select cases, local excision serves as a treatment option in place of chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMORADIATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="ANAL CANCER" id="related_to">
