    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgery and cancer treatment are closely interconnected, with surgery serving as a major therapeutic approach in oncology. Surgery is a form of cancer treatment that is used to remove cancerous tissue from the body. It is also one component of a broader cancer treatment plan, which may include other modalities such as radiation therapy, chemotherapy, or immunotherapy. In the context of head and neck cancers specifically, surgery is considered a complex form of cancer treatment due to the intricate anatomy of the region and the need for precise removal of tumors while preserving critical functions like breathing, swallowing, and speech. Additionally, discussions of surgery as a cancer treatment include attention to surgery side effects in cancer care, acknowledging that the procedure can have physical and functional impacts that must be managed before, during, and after treatment. Thus, surgery is both a distinct, direct method for excising cancer and an integral part of multidisciplinary cancer care, with its own risks and benefits that are weighed carefully by the medical team.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
