    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="DENTIST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">DENTIST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">It is important to tell your dentist about head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">DENTIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Head and neck cancer is a broad category of cancers that affect regions including the oral cavity, throat, larynx, sinuses, and salivary glands. Nasopharyngeal cancer is a specific type of head and neck cancer that arises in the nasopharynx, which is the upper part of the throat located behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. As a subtype of head and neck cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer shares the general characteristics of this disease group but is distinguished by its anatomical origin. Both descriptions confirm that nasopharyngeal cancer originates specifically in the nasopharynx and falls under the classification of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="OROPHARYNGEAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">OROPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Oropharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that arises in the oropharynx, which is part of the throat. Head and neck cancer is a broader category of malignancies, and oropharyngeal cancer represents a specific subtype originating in the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d12">OROPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="LARYNGEAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">LARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Laryngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, arising in the larynx/voice box</data>
      <data key="d12">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="HYPOPHARYNGEAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">HYPOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Head and neck cancer can occur in the hypopharynx, so hypopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">HYPOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="DENTAL EXAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">DENTAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Head and neck cancer treatment can significantly affect the jaw, teeth, and gums, making dental health a critical part of patient care. Because of these potential impacts, a dental exam is highly important for individuals undergoing or preparing for treatment. Regular dental cleanings and checkups are essential to monitor and maintain oral health, as head and neck cancer therapiessuch as radiation or surgerycan compromise the structures of the mouth and jaw. Dental exams help identify and manage complications early, reducing the risk of serious dental issues during and after cancer treatment. Thus, for head and neck cancer patients, consistent dental care is a vital component of overall treatment and long-term wellbeing.</data>
      <data key="d12">DENTAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is closely associated with head and neck cancer. As the most common type of mouth cancer, and since mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, SCC is a significant subtype within this broader category. Additionally, squamous cell carcinoma arising in the nasopharynx is also a form of head and neck cancer. Therefore, SCC can manifest in various locations within the head and neck region, including the oral cavity and nasopharynx, and is recognized as a major histological type of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="MOUTH CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">MOUTH CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, listed as cancer of the oral cavity</data>
      <data key="d12">MOUTH CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer treatment for head and neck cancer can affect fertility, so fertility preservation options should be discussed before starting treatment</data>
      <data key="d12">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" target="HEARING TEST" id="related_to">
