      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team plans and implements treatment, and systemic therapy is one of the treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Enrolling in a clinical trial for systemic therapy is an option.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Systemic therapy and clinical trials are both treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Systemic therapy may be given first for advanced cancers or cancer that has spread to lymph nodes; metastasis indicates advanced/spread cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Radiation therapy is sometimes given along with systemic therapy for unresectable cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Immunotherapy is a type of systemic therapy, meaning it is administered throughout the body rather than targeting a single localized area. As a systemic therapy, immunotherapy works by leveraging the bodys immune system to fight cancer. Specifically, it increases immune activity to help identify and destroy cancer cells. Thus, within the broader category of systemic therapy, immunotherapy is distinguished by its mechanism of enhancing the immune response to combat malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Systemic therapy is a type of cancer treatment using medicines that travel through the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d12">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COMPLICATION" target="TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COMPLICATION</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Treatment may cause treatment-related complications and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d12">COMPLICATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL SKIN TAGS" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ANAL SKIN TAGS</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Growths such as anal skin tags may be found and removed during surgery</data>
      <data key="d12">ANAL SKIN TAGS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A drug, device, or treatment approach found safe and effective in a clinical trial may be approved by the U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The Cancer Information Service assists individuals in locating appropriate clinical trials, including providing guidance on how to find them. It also answers cancer-related questions and addresses specific inquiries about clinical trials, such as their purpose, procedures, and eligibility. Additionally, the service offers contact information for further assistance, ensuring that individuals have access to the support they need when seeking information about cancer and clinical trial participation.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Both are resources that help patients find clinical trials; NLM provides clinicaltrials.gov and CIS offers direct assistance.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
