    </edge>
    <edge source="RECTAL CANCER" target="ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">APR is used for tumors in the lower rectum caused by rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">RECTAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" target="COLON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.</data>
      <data key="d12">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" target="POLYPS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
      <data key="d8">POLYPS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FAP causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum</data>
      <data key="d12">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" target="ADRENAL TUMORS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
      <data key="d8">ADRENAL TUMORS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.</data>
      <data key="d12">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLONOSCOPY" target="COLON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COLONOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Colon cancer often forms in polyps, which are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon. Colonoscopy is the medical procedure used to view and remove these polyps, and it plays a central role in both the detection and diagnosis of colon cancer. Most colon cancers are found during a colonoscopy, as the procedure allows direct visualization of the colon and the ability to biopsy or remove suspicious tissue. In some cases, another colonoscopy may be needed to confirm a diagnosis, particularly when initial findings require further evaluation or when polyp removal was incomplete. Thus, colonoscopy serves as both a screening and diagnostic tool for colon cancer, linking polyp detection and removal to the broader process of diagnosing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">COLONOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NON-METASTATIC CANCER" target="COLON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NON-METASTATIC CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Non-metastatic cancer is colon cancer that has not spread and is treated with surgery and sometimes chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d12">NON-METASTATIC CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NON-METASTATIC CANCER" target="COLECTOMY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NON-METASTATIC CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">COLECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Colectomy is surgery for non-metastatic colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">NON-METASTATIC CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NON-METASTATIC CANCER" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NON-METASTATIC CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is given after colectomy for all stage 3 cancers and some stage 2 cancers</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LYNCH SYNDROME" target="COLON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the diseases onset.</data>
      <data key="d12">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LYNCH SYNDROME" target="ADRENAL TUMORS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
      <data key="d8">ADRENAL TUMORS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Lynch syndrome is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.</data>
      <data key="d12">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LYNCH SYNDROME" target="HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
