    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="T-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.</data>
      <data key="d12">T-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) encompasses several subtypes, one of which is BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. This subtype is defined by the absence of the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, yet it shares a gene expression profile similar to BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL, hence the term "BCR::ABL1-like." It is recognized as a distinct subtype of B-ALL and is also the most common type of B-ALL overall. Thus, BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL represents the most frequently diagnosed form of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, characterized by its specific molecular and clinical features.</data>
      <data key="d12">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d12">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">When B-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in lymph nodes instead of bone marrow, the related condition is called B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d12">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
