      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Doctors ask about surgeries during medical history.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The medical history is a record of a patients symptoms, medicines, and related health information, which is shared with the care team during consultations. The care team relies on this medical history to help determine the best treatment plan for the patient. In turn, the patient provides details about symptoms and current medications during the medical history process, enabling the care team to make informed decisions. Together, the medical history and the care team form a collaborative loop: the patient supplies essential information, and the care team uses it to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST MRI" target="MAMMOGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A breast MRI may be used in addition to a mammogram</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGITAL MAMMOGRAPHY" target="TOMOSYNTHESIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIGITAL MAMMOGRAPHY</data>
      <data key="d8">TOMOSYNTHESIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Both are mammography methods: 2D digital mammography and 3D tomosynthesis</data>
      <data key="d12">DIGITAL MAMMOGRAPHY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SCREENING MAMMOGRAM" target="DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SCREENING MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A screening mammogram is an initial imaging exam used to detect potential breast abnormalities in individuals without symptoms. When a screening mammogram reveals an abnormal area, a diagnostic mammogram is performed as a follow-up. A diagnostic mammogram is used to take a closer look at the abnormal area found on the screening mammogram, providing more detailed images to help determine whether the finding is benign or requires further investigation. Together, the screening mammogram serves as the first-line detection tool, while the diagnostic mammogram provides focused evaluation of any suspicious findings identified during screening.</data>
      <data key="d12">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM" target="SCREENING MAMMOGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d8">SCREENING MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A diagnostic mammogram is different from a screening mammogram</data>
      <data key="d12">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
